/**
 * `pnpm music:build` — the bundled music library, synthesised from scratch.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL
 *
 * A tribute video that a family can share — email it to an aunt, put it on the
 * funeral home's page — cannot contain a commercial recording without a
 * per-song synchronisation licence. That leaves two honest options, and this
 * script is the first of them: original music, written and performed by nobody,
 * generated by arithmetic, released CC0, and therefore genuinely free of any
 * claim anyone could make against a grieving family.
 *
 * It is not a substitute for a real composer. It is a floor: eight quiet,
 * unobtrusive pieces that will not embarrass a room, with provenance nobody has
 * to take on trust because the provenance is this file.
 *
 * HOW IT WORKS
 *
 *  - a small additive synthesiser (wavetable oscillators, ADSR envelopes,
 *    per-partial decay) plays a slow chord progression at a known tempo;
 *  - four voices: bass, sustained pad, a plain melody, and — on some pieces —
 *    an arpeggio;
 *  - a Schroeder reverb (four combs, two allpasses) puts it in a room;
 *  - every random choice comes from a seeded PRNG, so re-running this produces
 *    byte-identical audio;
 *  - the result is written as 16-bit WAV and encoded to AAC by ffmpeg.
 *
 * Because the tempo is chosen rather than detected, each track's beat grid is
 * exact: the timing engine snaps slide changes to phrase boundaries that really
 * are where the music turns over.
 *
 * Output: content/music-library/<slug>/{audio.m4a,meta.json,LICENSE.txt}
 */
/* eslint-disable no-console */
